1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,626
A coach reads a runner's graph.

2
00:00:02,626 --> 00:00:04,854
An engineer reads a car's graph.

3
00:00:04,854 --> 00:00:07,184
A doctor reads a heartbeat graph.

4
00:00:07,584 --> 00:00:09,526
All of them ask two questions.

5
00:00:09,526 --> 00:00:11,044
How steep is the line?

6
00:00:11,044 --> 00:00:13,087
And how much space is under it?

7
00:00:13,537 --> 00:00:16,379
Aarav's bicycle meter
shows only his speed.

8
00:00:16,379 --> 00:00:18,656
It never shows how far he went.

9
00:00:19,006 --> 00:00:22,772
Yet he says he can find the
distance from the speed graph alone.

10
00:00:22,772 --> 00:00:24,188
Is that even possible?

11
00:00:24,688 --> 00:00:25,588
It is.

12
00:00:25,329 --> 00:00:29,126
By the end, you will read any
motion graph with two tools.

13
00:00:29,776 --> 00:00:31,343
Tool one is the slope.

14
00:00:31,693 --> 00:00:33,352
Slope means steepness.

15
00:00:33,352 --> 00:00:37,537
How much the line rises,
divided by how much time passes.

16
00:00:37,537 --> 00:00:38,762
Rise over run.

17
00:00:39,162 --> 00:00:40,805
Tool two is the area.

18
00:00:41,155 --> 00:00:44,745
Area means the space between
the line and the time axis.

19
00:00:44,745 --> 00:00:47,541
We find it like a rectangle or a triangle.

20
00:00:48,041 --> 00:00:51,278
Slope tells you a rate,
how fast something changes.

21
00:00:51,278 --> 00:00:54,091
Area tells you a total, how much piles up.

22
00:00:54,741 --> 00:00:57,794
Let's watch Aarav's ride
on our motion notebook.

23
00:00:58,144 --> 00:01:02,871
He starts from rest and speeds up for
four seconds, to six metres per second.

24
00:01:03,271 --> 00:01:07,189
Then he rides steady at six metres
per second for four seconds.

25
00:01:07,589 --> 00:01:10,059
Then he brakes, and stops in two seconds.

26
00:01:10,459 --> 00:01:11,886
Slow motion replay.

27
00:01:11,886 --> 00:01:16,685
While braking, velocity points forward,
but acceleration points backward.

28
00:01:17,185 --> 00:01:20,622
Now watch the blue space
under the velocity graph.

29
00:01:20,622 --> 00:01:23,359
It grows exactly as his distance grows.

30
00:01:23,759 --> 00:01:26,175
At the end, the area is forty two.

31
00:01:26,175 --> 00:01:27,893
He rode forty two metres.

32
00:01:27,893 --> 00:01:29,612
The area is the distance.

33
00:01:30,262 --> 00:01:32,439
Now let's see why, step by step.

34
00:01:32,789 --> 00:01:33,689
Step one.

35
00:01:33,641 --> 00:01:38,461
On a position time graph, slope is
the change in position over the change

36
00:01:38,461 --> 00:01:39,361
in time.

37
00:01:39,261 --> 00:01:40,590
That is velocity.

38
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:42,240
Step two.

39
00:01:42,222 --> 00:01:47,211
On a velocity time graph, slope is
the change in velocity over the change

40
00:01:47,211 --> 00:01:48,111
in time.

41
00:01:48,039 --> 00:01:49,630
That is acceleration.

42
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:51,397
Step three.

43
00:01:51,397 --> 00:01:55,717
The area under a velocity time
graph is velocity times time.

44
00:01:55,717 --> 00:01:57,353
That is displacement.

45
00:01:58,103 --> 00:01:59,068
Step four.

46
00:01:59,068 --> 00:02:03,907
The area under an acceleration time
graph is acceleration times time.

47
00:02:03,907 --> 00:02:06,305
That is the change in velocity.

48
00:02:07,055 --> 00:02:08,236
Check the units.

49
00:02:08,236 --> 00:02:11,327
Metres per second, times
seconds, gives metres.

50
00:02:11,327 --> 00:02:13,304
This area really is a length.

51
00:02:14,005 --> 00:02:15,045
Remember this.

52
00:02:15,045 --> 00:02:16,544
Slope gives the rate.

53
00:02:16,544 --> 00:02:18,042
Area gives the total.

54
00:02:18,742 --> 00:02:22,764
But why does area give distance,
even when the speed keeps changing?

55
00:02:23,114 --> 00:02:27,302
Cut the time into thin slices,
each just a tiny bit of time.

56
00:02:27,702 --> 00:02:30,762
In one thin slice, the
speed hardly changes.

57
00:02:30,762 --> 00:02:34,307
So the distance in it is
speed times that tiny time.

58
00:02:34,707 --> 00:02:38,637
And speed times that tiny time
is the area of that thin strip.

59
00:02:39,037 --> 00:02:43,033
Add up all the strips, and you
add up all the little distances.

60
00:02:43,033 --> 00:02:45,031
Total area, total displacement.

61
00:02:45,681 --> 00:02:46,775
Side by side.

62
00:02:46,775 --> 00:02:50,534
Here is a straight rising
line on a position time graph.

63
00:02:50,934 --> 00:02:54,181
Its slope is steady, so
the velocity is steady.

64
00:02:54,181 --> 00:02:55,454
Constant velocity.

65
00:02:55,854 --> 00:02:59,355
Now the same straight line
on a velocity time graph.

66
00:02:59,355 --> 00:03:02,470
Here a steady slope means
constant acceleration.

67
00:03:03,070 --> 00:03:04,000
Your turn.

68
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,756
A velocity time graph is a flat line.

69
00:03:06,756 --> 00:03:08,614
What is the acceleration?

70
00:03:08,614 --> 00:03:09,982
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:13,182 --> 00:03:14,082
Zero!

72
00:03:13,784 --> 00:03:15,786
A flat line has no slope.

73
00:03:15,786 --> 00:03:19,928
The velocity is not changing,
so the acceleration is zero.

74
00:03:20,578 --> 00:03:21,742
One more pair.

75
00:03:21,742 --> 00:03:26,234
The area under a velocity time
graph gives displacement, in metres.

76
00:03:26,634 --> 00:03:30,599
So what does the area under an
acceleration time graph give?

77
00:03:30,599 --> 00:03:32,880
Distance, or change in velocity?

78
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:34,127
Pause and think.

79
00:03:37,327 --> 00:03:38,791
Change in velocity!

80
00:03:38,791 --> 00:03:43,408
Metres per second squared, times
seconds, gives metres per second.

81
00:03:43,408 --> 00:03:44,384
A velocity.

82
00:03:45,034 --> 00:03:48,084
Now, one worked example,
from Aarav's ride.

83
00:03:48,434 --> 00:03:52,951
Find his acceleration in the first four
seconds, and his total displacement.

84
00:03:53,451 --> 00:03:55,299
Acceleration is the slope.

85
00:03:55,299 --> 00:03:57,745
The rise is six metres per second.

86
00:03:57,745 --> 00:03:59,551
The run is four seconds.

87
00:04:00,301 --> 00:04:02,994
Six divided by four is one point five.

88
00:04:02,994 --> 00:04:06,372
So a is one point five
metres per second squared.

89
00:04:07,122 --> 00:04:08,868
Displacement is the area.

90
00:04:08,868 --> 00:04:13,465
A trapezium, with parallel sides of
ten seconds and four seconds, and a

91
00:04:13,465 --> 00:04:14,599
height of six.

92
00:04:15,349 --> 00:04:18,896
Half of ten plus four,
times six, is forty two.

93
00:04:18,896 --> 00:04:20,930
Aarav rode forty two metres.

94
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:23,045
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:23,395 --> 00:04:26,453
Some velocity graphs
dip below the time axis.

96
00:04:26,453 --> 00:04:28,658
That part means moving backward.

97
00:04:29,008 --> 00:04:31,149
Many students add all the area.

98
00:04:31,149 --> 00:04:35,114
But for displacement, area below
the axis counts as negative.

99
00:04:35,114 --> 00:04:37,771
Only for distance do you add the sizes.

100
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:39,901
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:39,901 --> 00:04:44,831
Usually as a graph question: find the
acceleration, displacement or distance.

102
00:04:45,431 --> 00:04:46,634
Exam level now.

103
00:04:46,634 --> 00:04:49,581
A car moves at plus ten metres per second.

104
00:04:49,581 --> 00:04:54,098
Its acceleration is plus two for
five seconds, then minus four for the

105
00:04:54,098 --> 00:04:54,998
next five.

106
00:04:54,985 --> 00:04:57,290
Find its velocity at ten seconds.

107
00:04:57,290 --> 00:04:58,544
Pause and solve.

108
00:05:03,944 --> 00:05:05,351
The answer is A.

109
00:05:05,351 --> 00:05:07,140
Zero metres per second.

110
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:11,366
Area under the acceleration
graph is change in velocity.

111
00:05:11,366 --> 00:05:14,190
First part, two times five, plus ten.

112
00:05:14,190 --> 00:05:17,698
Second part, minus four
times five, minus twenty.

113
00:05:18,198 --> 00:05:20,169
Total change, minus ten.

114
00:05:20,169 --> 00:05:21,866
Add the starting ten.

115
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:23,672
Final velocity is zero.

116
00:05:24,272 --> 00:05:25,734
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:25,734 --> 00:05:27,237
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:30,437 --> 00:05:33,274
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:33,674 --> 00:05:34,574
One.

120
00:05:34,144 --> 00:05:37,470
What does the slope of a
position time graph tell you?

121
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:39,270
Two.

122
00:05:38,924 --> 00:05:43,391
Why does the area under a velocity
time graph give displacement?

123
00:05:44,291 --> 00:05:45,191
Three.

124
00:05:44,922 --> 00:05:48,813
When does the area below the
time axis count as negative?

125
00:05:49,913 --> 00:05:53,911
Next time: two buses side by
side look still to each other.

126
00:05:53,911 --> 00:05:54,811
Why?

127
00:05:54,418 --> 00:05:57,383
Find out in relative
motion in one dimension.
